From e3f65274868eb3b1ea2fb9bda33058204904e271 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Germano Caronni <gec@acm.org>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 20:49:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] =?UTF-8?q?tiles.pool:=20the=20pooled-evidence=20resid?=
 =?UTF-8?q?ue=20row=20=E2=80=94=20mean=20within=20a=20capture,=20sum=20acr?=
 =?UTF-8?q?oss?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The measured multi-scan law (CLAUDE.md, F1∘F6 replicated 3x) says
equal-weight combining beats best-single once the context detector has
de-correlated context bias. holo3 already obeys the CROSS-CAPTURE half:
`tiles._combine` sums each capture's post-context printed_llr cell by
cell, so `tiles.union` IS equal-weight cross-capture pooling and
`tiles.retry` already re-runs it at 150 iterations. Measured at the
codec, not read off the code (scratch probe, c3-k-r0550@7x7, sd 2.0
AWGN on the true field): at amp 2.0 capture A alone fails at bp150,
capture B alone fails, their sum certifies.

What holo3 does NOT pool is the WINDOW axis. Inside one capture
`freeze_page_owners` hands each cell whole to the best-held-out-loss
covering window and discards the rest, and windows overlap by
construction — 200 cells on a 160 step, so on the K sim's 448-cell page
the starts are (0, 160, 248) and over half the cells are covered twice.
Each window carries its own pose and its own calibration/context fit:
two windows over one cell are two de-correlated readouts of it.

tiles.pool keeps both. Per cell: the equal-weight MEAN of every reading
one capture holds (a mean, never a sum — those readings share photons,
and "never double-count the same physical capture" is standing law),
then the equal-weight SUM of the per-capture means, exactly as union
sums. Same probe, same noise, amp 1.2 through 1.6: single fails, union
fails, pool certifies; from 1.7 up both close.

Shape:
- subject = the union row's own vocabulary, (page, ty, tx, exact
  rational ppc), minted only where >=2 DISTINCT captures cover the tile
  at that rung. Own-capture pooling is structurally impossible: a
  capture's ladder is strictly ascending, one lane per ppc
  (MULTIRES-SPEC). No new alignment was invented.
- born from any ordinary tile attempt that did not certify (scan, union
  and the heavy retry — a retry150 failure is the strongest evidence
  that the residue needs a different assembly, not more iterations);
  never from its own facts, and `_retry_subjects` will not arm off a
  pool fact either, so there is no loop.
- re-arms through the declared reads and the version floor alone — it
  shares the union row's `reads` FUNCTION OBJECT, so the subscription is
  identical by construction and holo3/schedule.py derives its families
  correctly. No digests.
- fires at the heavy budget (150) and refuses when every cell was read
  once by every capture: the pooled cut is then bit-identical to the
  winner-take-all cut and that attempt has already been made.
- prices itself at 110 x its own fire ordinal, +2/rung: after the
  ordinary payload band and the cheap placement rungs, before the
  speculative searches (wide 120, cold 140/150) and refine (200). The
  engine is untouched except for the two registry lines.
- certificates flow through `tiles.attempt_tile` verbatim — K, cmy7 and
  cmy8 alike — so zero-wrong stays codec-enforced and the row is purely
  additive over the residue.

SCHEDULE.md's generated block is re-rendered from the descriptors
(`python -m holo3 schedule`), not hand-edited.

Suite green at this commit: 1396 passed, 376 skipped.

